---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Hurricane Helene, 2024"
  description="How the Florida Lantern Project responded to Hurricane Helene in September 2024 — the storm that turned a light show charity into a disaster relief organization."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="September 2024"
    title="Hurricane Helene"
    lede="The storm that changed what this organization is for."
  />

  <Section title="What happened">
    <p class="intro">
      Helene came up Florida's Gulf coast in late September 2024. At the time we
      were still Sylvan Ramble Lights — a holiday light show charity that raised
      money for other people's causes and had never run a relief operation.
    </p>
    <p>
      On 27 September we posted this:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="pull">
      <p>
        <strong>URGENT.</strong> We are stepping outside of our normal mission to
        provide urgent support for those impacted by Hurricane Helene. If you are
        in need of assistance or would like to donate to those in need, visit our
        website.
      </p>
      <p>
        100% of the funds raised will go directly to those in need, ensuring they
        receive immediate help during this challenging time.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      We had no disaster budget, no protocols, and no staff. What we had was a
      community that trusted us, a list of businesses who had said yes before, and
      a willingness to make phone calls.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" title="What we did">
    <ul class="did">
      <li><strong>Direct financial assistance</strong> — 100% pass-through, no administrative deduction</li>
      <li><strong>Emergency shelter</strong> — through hotel partnerships negotiated in the days before landfall</li>
      <li><strong>Transportation</strong> — for families who had no way to leave</li>
      <li><strong>Cleanup support</strong> — volunteer crews in the days afterwards</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Then it happened again">
    <p class="intro">
      Nine days after Helene's landfall, Hurricane Milton formed. It reached Florida
      on 9 October.
    </p>
    <p>
      Families we had just helped were hit a second time before insurance adjusters
      could reach the first round of damage. One of them was Amy — a single mother
      with two children, two cats and a dog, whose house Helene had flooded and
      whose car it had totalled.
    </p>
    <p><a class="btn" href="/response/milton-2024">Hurricane Milton &rarr;</a></p>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" title="What we learned">
    <ul class="learned">
      <li>
        <strong>Speed beats process.</strong> Every hour spent designing an
        intake procedure was an hour a family spent not knowing if help was
        coming. We got faster by deciding sooner, not by planning better.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Existing relationships are the whole asset.</strong> We had no
        money. What we had was eight years of local businesses knowing who we
        were, and that turned out to be convertible into hotel rooms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storms don't queue.</strong> Planning for one event at a time is
        planning wrong.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .intro { font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .pull {
    margin: var(--space-md) 0; padding: var(--space-md);
    background: var(--color-surface); border-left: 4px solid var(--flp-red-500);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .pull p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .did, .learned { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-sm); margin-top: var(--space-md); }
  .did li, .learned li { border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); padding-left: var(--space-sm); max-width: var(--measure); }
</style>
